The file is written from the timeline as it was when you confirmed it.

### While it says Preparing

Some of what a file holds has to be made first, so the export waits for it:

- The masks of every [Remove Background](/effects/remove-background/#exports) clip in the range, with no time limit.
- Each clip's cursor, from its [cursor file](/recording/cursor-files/).
- The measured loudness of every clip with a gate, leveller or de-esser, for up to five minutes.
- The rendered sound of speed changes, Voice Isolation and voice effects.

GIF and APNG files have no sound, so they don't wait for the last two. You can cancel during any of these waits. [Waiting for what it needs](/export/what-an-export-includes/#waiting-for-what-it-needs) lists which clips each wait covers, and what happens if something can't be made.

## Cancel a running export

Choose **Cancel**. The button greys out while the export stops, then the sheet closes and the partly written file is removed.

:::caution
If you chose to replace an existing file in the save panel, that file is gone once you cancel. Cancelling doesn't bring it back.
:::

## The finish report

When an export finishes, the status line names the file and says what the sound measured:
It keeps real transparency, so a pixel can be any fraction of opaque, and the [footnote](/export/export-sheet/#the-footnote) ends in `alpha`. It's written at 8 bits a component and labelled BT.709.

A viewer that knows nothing of APNG shows the first frame.

## The 600-frame limit

An animated GIF or APNG holds at most 600 frames. That's 48 seconds at GIF's 12.5 fps, or 24 seconds at APNG's 25 fps. The readout shows the count while you're choosing, but the sheet doesn't stop you. Once you've picked where to save, a longer export stops before it writes anything:

> An animated GIF is limited to 600 frames and this range is 720. Shorten the range, or lower the export frame rate.

For GIF the way out is a shorter range, because every fixed rate in the **Frame rate:** popup is higher than 12.5 fps. An APNG can go down to 24 or 23.976 fps, which gains you about a second.

## Fitting inside the size

The **Size:** popup lists **GIF default** or **APNG default** first, and it's the default for those two formats and no others. **GIF default** is 480 × 270 and **APNG default** is 960 × 540, each fitted to the canvas's shape.

Every row, and a **Custom…** size, is a box the picture fits inside. It keeps its shape and is never enlarged, and nothing is letterboxed. The rows show the size the file will have. A 1080 × 1920 project's GIF default is 152 × 270.
